--------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. Purpose

Grey M1 solver a la Radice et al. 2022 [arXiv:2111.14858].

This code needs 2 ghost cells on unigrid and 4 ghost cells with AMR.

Optional reconstructed radiation HLLE transport
---------------------------------------------

The default nuX_M1::transport_flux = "lax-friedrichs" retains the original
limited cell-centred Lax-Friedrichs flux kernels. Set transport_flux = "hlle"
to select the independent reconstructed-face solver. hlle_limiter = "mc"
(default) or "minmod" selects second-order TVD slopes. The Lax-Friedrichs
parameters minmod_theta, mindiss, and sawtooth_force_full_dissipation do not
affect HLLE. Both paths
require two transport ghost cells (additional AMR/GR stencil requirements
still apply). These selection parameters are not steerable.
The HLLE path supports Minerbo, Kershaw, and Eddington; the fixed "thin"
closure is rejected for HLLE because its general Jacobian is singular at
F=0 and can be superluminal away from free streaming. The variable closures
describe free streaming without that fixed-model pathology. The Lax-Friedrichs
path's existing support for the thin model is unchanged.
Zero radiation floors are supported: with rad_E_floor=rad_N_floor=0,
E=N=Fi=0 is an exact vacuum with zero reconstructed ratios and zero physical
flux. Variable closures use a conservative causal fan at vacuum; Eddington
uses its fixed-v isotropic regular-limit fan. A zero energy floor requires
a zero number floor, and states with E=0 but positive N or nonzero Fi are
rejected because their moment ratios do not define finite interface states.
Raw HLLE stencil moments and reconstructed face ratios must be finite before
flooring, slope limiting or cone repair. These operations must not hide NaNs
or infinities; invalid inputs abort through the existing release-safe handler.
Finite-state floors and realizability repairs are unchanged.

Following section 3.1 of https://arxiv.org/html/2304.09168v1, reconstruct
(N/E, Fx/E, Fy/E, Fz/E, E), with physical E=rE/sqrt(gamma), rather than
reconstructing densitized components independently. Evaluate lapse, shift,
and spatial metric with the existing nuX vertex-to-cell interpolation, then
average adjacent cells to form one common face geometry. The fiducial
velocity is centered at the face and repaired in that metric; W is recomputed
from it, never independently interpolated. Reconstructed Fi/E is projected
onto the realizable cone in the face metric. Recompute the selected closure
on both sides; do not reconstruct a stale cell-centred pressure tensor.

Conserved components and their numerical/physical fluxes consistently use
the existing order (rN,rFx,rFy,rFz,rE). Multiply face physical moments and
fluxes by the same face sqrt(gamma). The number current is n(u^mu+H^mu/J),
with Gamma=alpha*(u^0+H^0/J) from that same regularized current, including
floor states. Thus its coordinate advection speed is alpha*fnu^d/Gamma.
No diagnostic or face grid functions are added; the HLLE schedule also omits
the Lax-Friedrichs-only nu_flux_x/y/z storage and physical-flux kernels. Do not
request those scratch variables as output with transport_flux="hlle".

Speeds and corrections to the reference formulas:

* Use signed sL=min(0,lambdaL-,lambdaR-) and
  sR=max(0,lambdaL+,lambdaR+). The consistent HLLE expression is
  (sR*FL-sL*FR+sL*sR*(UR-UL))/(sR-sL), not Eq. (62)'s printed state sum
  and speed sum. A zero-width fan uses the centered physical flux.
* Do NOT interpolate thick/thin eigenvalues as in Eq. (57): that estimate
  need not enclose the actual closure Jacobian. For example, static Minerbo
  f=|F|/E=0.5 aligned with the normal has lambda+=0.7541494487, while the
  interpolated endpoint estimate gives only 0.6460308504.
* Eddington uses the exact fixed-v thick-system roots, with p=alpha*v^d
  and the advective root -beta^d+p in the lower MIN and upper MAX. This
  corrects the printed p=alpha*v^d/W and two MINs in Eqs. (58)-(60).
  The roots reproduce (v +/- 1/sqrt(3))/(1 +/- v/sqrt(3)) in 1D flat space.
* For a static fiducial observer, differentiate the actual variable closure
  Pij/E=(1-chi)*deltaij/2+(3*chi-1)*ni*nj/2 in a local orthonormal frame.
  The normal cosine is mu=F^d/(sqrt(gamma^{dd})*|F|), including off-diagonal
  metrics. Rotation about the normal reduces its four eigenvalues to a
  three-by-three cubic plus the transverse root (3*chi-1)*mu/(2*f).
  Minerbo uses all four roots, with explicit isotropic and streaming limits.
  Kershaw factors analytically: two roots f*mu and the roots
  2*f*mu/3 +/- sqrt((1-f^2)/3+f^2*mu^2/9). Transform orthonormal roots with
  -beta^d+alpha*sqrt(gamma^{dd})*lambda. Static face chi is evaluated
  directly from f (J=E and |H|=|F|), so fluxes and their differentiated
  analytic closure agree without root-finding tolerance inconsistencies.
* Nearly multiple/roundoff-ambiguous Minerbo cubics, and moving-frame
  Minerbo/Kershaw states, use the conservative coordinate light cone
  -beta^d +/- alpha*sqrt(gamma^{dd}), not an invented moving eigenformula.
  Well-separated cubic roots receive a roundoff guard. The fallback can
  make the low-order local fan an LLF fan; reconstruction and diffusion
  correction remain active. Include the closure-derived number-current
  advection eigenvalue in all final bounds. Thus the exact-static/exact-
  Eddington cases and conservative causal fallback are explicit, not a
  disclaimer attached to a knowingly underbounded interpolated fan.
* Set A=min(1,1/(mean(kappa)*dl)), dl=dx/sqrt(gamma^{dd}), so opacities
  multiply proper normal distance, including non-diagonal spatial metrics.
  kappa=abs_1+scat_1 controls relaxation of H/J and therefore transports
  both energy and the tied number current. Energy and number use A only
  on the HLLE state-jump viscosity. Momentum uses
  A^2*F_HLLE+(1-A^2)*(FL+FR)/2. The extra outer 1/2 printed in Eq. (64)
  is omitted: it violates constant-state consistency already at A=1.
  At high opacity this removes opacity-independent artificial diffusion;
  collision integration still must resolve the coupled diffusion limit.

Validation status and proposed checks
------------------------------------

No build or evolution was performed for this change. nuX_Tests now contains
future executable assertions for constant-state consistency, signed/upwind
and zero-speed fans, A/A^2 scaling, reconstruction slopes, moving thick
eigenvalues, the f=0.5 counterexample, oblique/transverse static Jacobian
spectra, streaming/isotropic/cubic-degeneracy limits, moving-frame causal
fallback, non-diagonal GR face geometry,
component mapping, densitization, and realizability. They run with the
existing nuX_Tests/test/unit_tests.par after an authorized build.

nuX_M1/par/nuX_M1_hlle_smoke.par is a small periodic, moving Gaussian smoke
case. It is not a regression result or a demonstrated convergence test.
After building and running are authorized, check positivity/realizability
and periodic integral conservation, repeat with kappa_scat_nue=1e3 and
closure="Eddington", and measure diffusion/advection convergence under
resolution and opacity changes. The established beam, crossing-beam,
adv_velocity_jump, and Kerr-Schild parfiles can be copied and opted into
HLLE for directional/curved-space checks. Existing Lax-Friedrichs regression
parfiles and their reference data remain unchanged. AMR transport retains the
existing cell-local divergence infrastructure; no new refluxing algorithm
is supplied or claimed.

A. Acknowledgements

This code is ported from THC_M1 (https://bitbucket.org/FreeTHC/thccore/src/master/) of Radice et al. 
